  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms continue across western WI early this\nmorning ahead of a wavy cold front located from around Lake\nMille Lacs southward to Buffalo and Hutchinson to St. James.\nCooler and drier air is slowly filtering into western MN, along\nwith clearing skies. Some wind gusts are noted in the CAA\nregime but they are still relatively light. Winds will ease\nthroughout the day as a high pressure ridge axis builds in\nmaking for a pleasant day as temperatures climb into the 70s.\n\nA short wave will rotate southeast across MN into WI Sunday. \nThis may bring a chance of showers across southern MN into IA \nand SD, but dry high pressure to the north will limit the \nnorthern extent of any precip. The high will slide east to the \nGreat Lakes Sunday night and warm air advection will begin \nacross the Upper Midwest as a potent trough advances into the \nnorthern Plains. Very strong moisture advection will be brought \non by a 55 kt LLJ Monday and pwats are expected to increase to \njust short of 2 inches. Isentropic lift will allow widespread \nshowers to develop across the region, but the trough will swing \nthrough quickly and limit the residence time of the activity. In\naddition, the surface warm front will remain well to the south \nacross southwestern IA and keep the more efficient thunderstorms\nanchored there. Models continue to trend down on QPF, ranging\nfrom a GEFS mean around a 1/3 inch on the low side to 1/2-3/4\ninch with most of the other ensemble systems. Given the lift \nand moisture in place, thinking at least a half inch is\nprobable. If heavier rates sustain themselves for a little \nwhile longer then we'll see numbers closer to an inch. A front\nwill drape itself from northern WI to southeast MN and \nnorthwest IA Monday night. The entrance region of the LLJ will \nstrengthen across IA and impinge on the front allowing for \nanother round of showers and storms to develop along it before \nsliding southeast Tuesday. The highest QPF will be found in this\ncorridor with 1-2 inches expected.\n\nFor the rest of next week, zonal flow will continue with mid\nlevel ridging remaining anchored in place across the south. A \nnearly stationary front will draped from the central Plains to \nmid Mississippi Valley and will keep the hot and humid air well \nto the south. It will also act as a focus for numerous rounds of\nshowers and thunderstorms. At this time the odds of being under\nthe influence of dry high pressure outweigh the prospects of \nany significant rain associated with the front for us.\nSeasonably cool air will remain in place until further notice\nwith highs in the 60s and low 70s and lows in the 40s or 50s.\nSome 30s are possible across northern MN and northern/central WI\nat times.",
